<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josquin des Prez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1521">1521</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Josquin, Josquin Desprez, Josquin Lebloitte dit des Prez</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josquin des Prez was a French or Franco-Flemish composer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and a central figure of the Franco-Flemish School, born around 1450 in present-day northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. A singer by training, he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a>, Cardinal Ascanio Sforza, Popes Innocent VIII and Alexander VI, Louis XII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ercole-i-d-este">Ercole I d'Este</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, for whom he wrote the Missa Hercules Dux Ferrariae. His masses, motets and chansons developed an imitative polyphony of independent voices closely tied to the text, and many were printed by Ottaviano Petrucci. From 1504 he was provost of the collegiate church of Notre-Dame at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conde-sur-lescaut">Condé-sur-l'Escaut</a>, and he died in 1521.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez.jpg" alt="Josquin des Prez" width="330" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josquin_woodcut.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
